  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued unsettled and humid today. Widespread\nshowers and a few thunderstorms early, then scattered afternoon\nshowers/storms possible. Mainly dry with skies clearing tonight.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates a sfc warm front from northern\nVA to the MD eastern shore, with a sfc trough farther south\nacross the VA/NC piedmont. The next batch of showers has\ndeveloped across south-centra//central VA along the sfc trough,\nwith another area of showers and some tstms across the NE\nportions of the FA near the warm front. There is decent low\nlevel wind shear near the warm front and less CIN so will be \nwatching the eastern shore closely through the next few hrs for\nany potential stronger storms or quick spin- up circulations.\nOtherwise, just locally heavy rain through around 8-9 AM (with\nPWATs 2\"+) before shower/storms diminish and push offshore and\noff to the NE as the warm front lifts away from the local area.\nExpect a lull in the precip for a few hrs, with some\nredevelopment likely this aftn as the early AM clouds lift and\nscatter out. Models show ML CAPE this aftn of 1000-1500 J/Kg,\nhighest E of I-95. Highest chances will be across the SE, where\n40-60% PoPs are forecast for a few hrs, though the CAMs also\nhave honed in on some development across the mountains, so will\nhave 20-30% PoPs moving in from the NW as well. There is no SPC\noutlook today but an isolated stronger storm is possible, along\nwith locally heavy rain. Highs today generally in the mid-upper\n80s. Drying out after sunset, with lows mostly in the upper 60s\nto lower 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and significantly \nlower humidity Monday-Wednesday. Temperatures warm back above normal\nlate in the week an upper ridge builds back into the region. Next\nchance for rain Fri-Sat. \n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW on Monday as a strong upper level \ntrough dives SE from the Great Lakes and pushes off the NE and mid-\nAtlantic coast Monday night, with an upper level ridge slowly \nexpanding NE from the lower MS Valley. This looks strong enough to \ndrive the sfc cold front well south of the CWA Monday, and bring \nsignificantly lower dew points that are more typical of September. \nThe models continue to depict a strong shortwave passing through\nthe region on Monday, so do expect to see a 3-6 hr window where\nclouds increase and even a few isolated showers develop on \nMonday before the much drier air surges in from the NNW during \nthe afternoon. Best chance for seeing a few showers with the\nfront will be across the far SE where higher dew pts will linger\nthrough the mid aftn hrs. Otherwise, becoming breezy in the \nimmediate wake of the front with significantly drier air and \ndeep mixing aided by the shortwave aloft. With the N-NE win \ndirection, expect to see some gusts to 25-30 mph at the coast in\nSE VA and NE NC. Highs will be in the low-mid 80s for most.\n\nAs high pressure builds in behind the front Monday night, a \npleasant night is expected Monday night/Tue AM as winds diminish\ninland (staying elevated near the coast). Even the NBM now shows\nlows into the 50s for most areas away from the coast, so expect\nto see low-mid 50s across most of the piedmont, and 55-60F\nelsewhere (locally 65-70F at the coast). The upper level ridge\nbecomes nearly stationary across the TN Valley Tue-Wed as a weak\nupper level trough develops off the SE Coast, with some models\nplacing this feature as far south as Florida. This pattern will\nallow strong sfc high pressure (~1030 mb), to set up from off \nthe New England coast SW into the Carolina coastal plain through\nmidweek. Highs will stay mostly in the low-mid 80s through Wed \nwith lows in the 50s to lower 60s (locally warmer at the \nimmediate coast). The models now mostly have the upper tough to\nour S/SE potentially amplifying late in the week which would\nretrograde the ridge back towards the lower MS Valley. Overall,\na decent amount of uncertainty as to how this evolves, but the\nforecast will continue to show temperatures climbing back to \nabove normal for mid- September, with some low-end rain chances\nFri-Sat.",
